rsion clears the provenance refusal, and that transition - from 'requires captured clean Git provenance' to an ordinary downstream shape error - is the evidence the fix works. POWER PROVEN BY TWO INJECTIONS: removing the call entirely reproduces the historical state (test RED, 10 dirty paths), and re-applying the too-narrow `.kontourai/flow-agents` scope - my own rejected first attempt - is also caught (test RED, 1 dirty path). Byte-identical restore after each (git hash-object == git rev-parse HEAD:<path>), then green. The behavioural assertion is that the tree is CLEAN after a run writes state, not that the ignore file has particular contents; a contents assertion would have passed against both rejected versions. A third assertion pins that an existing user-authored .kontourai/.gitignore is preserved verbatim (#1238 class). DISCLOSED GAPS, prominently: this fix removes the provenance blocker and does NOT make tests-evidence land. The measured run still ends at 12 invocations with 3 refusals (25%), reaching `verify`; the remaining refusals are the acceptance-criterion binding and the merge-ready expectation set, both untouched here. I observed what looked like a contradiction between two validators (one reporting 'expected: none', the other demanding one criterion per declared criterion) but could NOT reproduce it in a clean run - both variants give the same expectation-binding error - so it is recorded here as unreproduced rather than filed as a defect. The instrument models the criterion-discovery cost by probing with a knowingly wrong id, because the declared id is absent from both `workflow status` and state.json and is only ever named in a refusal. Registered in CHECKS, LANE_INTEGRATION_COVERAGE, evals/run.sh and .github/workflows/ci.yml, manifest membership confirmed. Scoped to this author and branch only.",
